full_judgment:
Md Shohrowardi, J : The Assessee-applicant filed this reference application under section 160(1) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 challenging the legality of the order dated 29-10-2015 passed by the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench-3, Dhaka in Income Tax Appeal No. 165 of 2015-2016 (Assessment year 2014-2015) and formulated the following questions of law; 
A. On the facts and in the circumstances whether the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench-3, Dhaka is justified in law in upholding or maintaining disallowances of the expenses of the applicant in view of section 35(3) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984? 
B. On the facts and in the circumstances whether the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench-3 Dhaka is justified in law in estimating receipt/income, sales of medicine and rate of GP by addition together with sale of fixed assets in view of section 35(3) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984? 
C. On the facts and in the circumstances whether the Taxes Appellate Tribunal is justified in law in upholding the order of the Commissioner of Taxes (Appeal) who passed his order without complying with the provisions of section 155(5) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984? 
D. On the facts and in the circumstances whether the Taxes Appellate Tribunal is justified in law in not calling for documents and evidence from the Assessee-Applicant on particular heads of income and expenditure before disposing of the appeal in view of section 159(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984? 

2. Relevant facts for disposal of this reference application are that the assessee-applicant is a regular assessee under the Taxes Circle-201 (Companies), Taxes Zone-10, Dhaka having TIN: 035-200-3766/Circle-201 /Taxes Zone-10, Dhaka who submitted return to the Deputy Commissioner of Taxes (Shortly, the DCT) for the assessment year 2014-2015 (Accounting year 1-7-2013 to 30-6-2014) showing total income of Taka 4,71,49,763 supported by audited accounts containing profit and loss accounts, trading accounts and balance sheet duly certified by the chartered accountant. The DCT by order of assessment dated (undated) disallowed some expenses and enhanced the total sales against which the assessee-applicant filed আয়কর আপীল পত্র-৫৬৮/র্কো-২০১/কঃঅঃ-১০/২০১৪-২০১৫ before the Commissioner of Taxes (Appeal), Taxes Zone-4, Dhaka who by order dated 19-5-2015 modified the order of the DCT and allowed the appeal in part. Thereafter the assessee-applicant filed Income Tax Appeal No. 165 of 2015-2016 before the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench-3, Dhaka who by order dated 29-10-2015 modified the order of the first appellate authority. 

3. The Assessee-applicant filed supplementary affidavit stating that the assessee-applicant furnished all necessary documents and evidences before the DCT but the DCT without considering the documents regarding expenses, rent and sales disallowed the expenses on different heads and enhanced the total sales and income and thereby assessed the income of the assessee-applicant on surmise and conjuncture and illegally imposed taxes upon the assessee applicant and the first appellate authority and the tribunal below did not consider documents submitted before the DCT. By filing a supplementary-affidavit, the assessee-applicant filed the following documents as regards expenses and sales which were also filed before the DCT; 
a. Seat Rent Register; 
b. Oxygen Charges Register; 
c. OT Charges Register; 
d. Dressing Charges Register; 
e. Dialysis Charges Register; 
f. Pathology Register; 
g. X-Ray Register; 
h. ECG Register; 
i. Ultrasonography Register; 
J. X-Ray Expenditure Ledger including vouchers; 
h. All supporting documents of cleaning and maintenance; 
i. All documents relating to conveyance; 
J. All bills and vouchers and documents of Tiffin and refreshment; 
k. All bills and vouchers and documents of business promotions; 
1. All bills and vouchers and documents of repairing and maintenance; 
m. All bills and vouchers and documents of printing and stationary; 
n. All bills of Newspaper; 
o. All bills and voucher and relevant documents of pathology expenses; 
p. All bills, vouchers and documents of OT Maintenance; 
q. All invoice of Oxygen purchase; 
r. All bills, vouchers and documents of diet; 
s. Sales register/Sales report of Medicine. 

4. The respondent filed affidavit-in-reply stating that after filling the return, the DCT was not objectively satisfied with the documents and the evidence produced by the assessee-applicant as those documents were not properly maintained. Thereafter, DCT served notice under sections 79/82 of Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 upon the assessee applicant. Despite service of statutory notice, the assessee-applicant did not produce the documents in support of his return although the assessee-applicant maintained computer generated patient register of its own without approval of the National Board of Revenue. Thereafter considering the documents submitted by the assessee-applicant and assigning good reason the DCT rejected the books of accounts audited by the chartered accountants. 

5. The learned Advocate Mr Abdus Salam Mamun appearing on behalf of the assessee-applicant submits that at the time of hearing before the DCT, the assessee-applicant submitted all relevant documents for his consideration and at the time of hearing, the representative of the assessee-company was present and presented the relevant documents before the DCT to allow the expenses and sales. But the DCT without considering those documents disallowed the expenses and illegally enhanced sales. He further submits that in the memo of appeal filed before the First Appellate Authority and the Tribunal below, the assessee-applicant has taken specific ground regarding non-consideration of the documents filed by the assessee-applicant before the DCT regarding expenses and sales, but the appellate authorities without considering those documents rejected the books of account audited by the chartered accountants without assigning reason and modified the order of DCT without any objective basis. He also submits that the First Appellate Authority is empowered under section 155(5) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 to make an enquiry or call for such particular regarding expenses and sales of the company but the First Appellate Authority without exercising its discretion mechanically modified the order of DCT. The Taxes Appellate Tribunal is also empowered under section 159 (2) of the Income Tax Ordinance,1984 to call for any particular regarding expenses and sales of the assessee-applicant or he may cause further inquiry to be made by the DCT, but the Taxes Appellate Tribunal in the same fashion without exercising judicial discretion under section 159 (2) of Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 modified the order of First Appellate Authority without any objective basis. He finally submits that complying with provision of section 161(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 the assessee-applicant filed all the relevant documents before this court which were also filed before the DCT. Therefore, this reference application should be sent back to the Taxes Appellate Tribunal to hear the matter afresh to consider those documents. 

6. The learned Deputy Attorney-General Ms. Mahfuza Begum appearing along with learned AAG Mr Ali Akbor Khan submits that the DCT is legally empowered under sections 35(4) and 30A of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 to reject the books of accounts audited by the chartered accountants assigning reasons and disallow the expenses on non-verifiability ground. She further submits that admittedly the notice under sections 79 and 83(1) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 was served upon the assessee-applicant but the assessee company did not file documents before the DCT. She also submits that the books of accounts audited by the chartered accountants is not a sacrosanct and no illegality was committed by the DCT and by the appellate authorities in modifying the order passed by the DCT. She finally submits that although there is no bar in sending the reference applicant on remand to the Taxes Appellate Tribunal but the Taxes Appellate Tribunal and the assessing authorities relying on the documents submitted by the assessee-company made the assessment. Therefore, in the facts and circumstances of the case, the assessee-applicant failed to make out a case of remand.

7. We have considered the submission of the learned Advocate Mr Abdus Salam Mamun who appeared on behalf of the applicant and the learned Deputy Attorney-General Ms Mahfuza Begum who appeared on behalf of the respondent. We have also perused reference application and affidavit in reply filed by the respondent. 

8. The issue involved in the instant reference application as to whether the DCT is empowered under sections 35(4) and 30A of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 to make disallowance of expenses or deduction from any claim made by the assessee in the trading, loss or profit account and reject the books of accounts audited by the chartered accountants. Further question involved as to whether exercising the reference jurisdiction under section 160 of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 this Court is empowered to send the reference application to the Taxes Appellate Tribunal or any authority on remand and as to whether this Court is legally bound to answer the question raised in the reference-application. 

9. On perusal of the order of assessment dated (undated) passed by the DCT and the First Appellate Authority it appears that after service of notice under sections 79 and 83(1) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 the representative of the assessee-company appeared before the DCT and submitted documents regarding expenses and sales and thereafter the DCT assessed the return under section 83(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 and made disallowances of the expenses on different heads and rejected the sales shown in the return by the assessee-applicant. The First Appellate Authority by order dated 19-5-2015 made some modification holding that the DCT made the assessment on presumption and enhanced the tax liability. 

10. On perusal of the order dated 29-10-2015 passed by the Taxes Appellate Tribunal it appears that considering the departmental inspection report, the Taxes Appellate Tribunal modified the order passed by First Appellate Authority holding that the DCT made estimate on non-verifiability ground, and ultimately modified the order passed by the First Appellate Authority considering the good will and growth of the business of the assessee-company. At the time of hearing before the DCT, the assessee-company filed documents regarding expenses and sales, and at the time of hearing before the appellate authorities the representative of the assessee-company submitted that the DCT did not consider the documents relating to expenses and sales. 

11. On scrutiny of the order passed by the First Appellate Authority and the Tribunal below it reveals that at the time of passing their orders they completely remain silent as regard the submission made by the assessee-company regarding non-consideration of documents filed before the DCT. 

12. Exercising discretion under sections 35(4) and 30A of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 the DCT is empowered to reject the books of accounts audited by the chartered accountants and make disallowance or deduction from any claim made by assessee in the trading account, loss or profit account specifying reason for such disallowance or deduction as the audited books of account are not sacrosanct. But the DCT is not authorized to reject the sales and disallow expenses on conjecture and surmises. In the instant case, the DCT rejected the expenses due to lack of documents but he did not assigned any reason for rejecting sales audited by the chartered accountant. 

13. All the documents regarding expenses lie with the assessee. After service of notice under section 83(1) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 if the assessee failed to produce relevant documents regarding expenses, the DCT is empowered to disallow expenses assigning reason. The DCT is not legally empowered to enhance sales or estimate receipts of income without any objective basis. If the DCT is of the prima-facie opinion that the assessee suppressed documents regarding sales and concealed income, he may cause for an inquiry under section 83(2) of the said Ordinance to collect other evidence and assess the total income of the assessee and determine the sum payable by him. 

14. In the case of Commissioner of Taxes vs Conference and Exhibition Management Service Ltd, Civil Appeal No. 101 of 2013 our Apex Court considered the decisions reported in 28 DLR (AD) 141, 34 DLR (AD) 298, 58 DLR 531 and also examined the power of DCT provided in section 35 of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 and held that; 
"It is our view that section 35(3) of the Ordinance indeed directs the assessee to furnish a copy of the trading account, profit and loss account and the balance sheet of the income year certified by a Chartered Accountant, but that does not obviate the requirement to provide evidence in support of the claims made by the assessee. We reiterate the finding of the High Court Division in the decision reported in 58 DLR 531 that accounts audited by a firm of Chartered Accountants cannot be said to be sacrosanct. When the tax authority indicates that any claims are disallowed on account of lack of verifiable evidence, it is incumbent upon the assessee to satisfy the tax authority by providing necessary supporting evidence. The provision in section 35(3) of the Ordinance, 1984 does not exonerate the assessee from supplying evidence in support of the claims for allowances/deductions." 

15. On a bare reading of section 155 of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 it reveals that before disposing an appeal, the Appellate Joint Commission or the Commissioner (Appeals) is empowered under section 155(5) of the said Ordinance to make such enquiry as he thinks fit or call for such particulars as he may require respecting the matters arising in appeals or cause further enquiry to be made by the DCT. First Appellate authority is also empowered under section 155(6) of the said Ordinance to allow the applicant to produce any documents or evidence in support of his return. In the instant case, the First Appellate Authority did not exercise his discretion as provided in section 155(5) of the said Ordinance passing an order for inquiry or calling for the documents submitted before the DCT. 

16. Under section 159(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 the Taxes Appellate Tribunal is empowered to call for such particulars as it may require respecting the matter arising in the appeal or cause further inquiry to be made by the DCT. On scrutiny of the order of assessment (undated) passed by DCT it reveals that after service of notice under section 79/83(1) of the said Ordinance the assessee-application submitted the required documents and no notice under section 83(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 was served upon the assessee to produce further evidence in support of the return. Although the assessee applicant specifically submitted before the Taxes Appellate Tribunal to consider the documents submitted before the DCT but the Taxes Appellate Tribunal did not exercise discretion as provided in section 159(2) of the said Act calling documents submitted before the DCT or making further enquiry to be made by the DCT. The assessee applicant is also entitled under section 155(6) and section 159(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 to submit evidence in support of his return before the First Appellate authority and the Taxes Appellate Tribunal respectively. 

17. The above view of this Court lends support from the view of our Apex court made in the case of Commissioner of Taxes vs Conference and Exhibition Management Service Ltd, Civil Appeal No. 101 of 2013 wherein his Lordship Mr Muhammad Iman Ali, J observed that; 
"From the order of the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, we do not find that the assessee furnished any supporting documents even at the appellate stage." 

18. In the instant case, the DCT rejected the expenses due to lake of documents. The learned Advocate for the assessee-applicant submits that the DCT and appellate authorities without considering the documents submitted before the DCT made the assessment imposing tax liability against the assessee-company. 

19. On a bare reading of section 161 of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 it appears that exercising the reference jurisdiction under section 161(2) of the said Ordinance this court is legally empowered to pass any order directing the Taxes Appellate Tribunal to consider the documents filed before the DCT before making assessment under section 83(2) of the said Ordinance or filed before any appellate authority or allow the assessee an opportunity to produce relevant documents or call for the documents already filed before the DCT or First Appellate Authority. This power is implicit in section 161 read with section 94(3) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984. 

20. The above view of this court lends supports from the case reported in AIR 1955 SC 65, 47 DLR 224, 13 BLC 593, 59 DLR 463, 57 DLR 756 and unreported decisions made in ITRA No. 95 of 2002 and ITRA No. 140 and 141 of 2011. 

21. As regards the duty upon this court regarding answering the question either in affirmative or in negative, we hold that at the time of filing the reference application the applicant shall file documents as referred to in section 160(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984. Exercising reference jurisdiction under section 160 of the said Ordinance this court is bound to give answer to the question raised in the reference application subject to compliance of mandatory provision of section 160(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 by the applicant and that the question raised in the reference application filed under section 160 of the said Ordinance has arisen out of the order passed by the Tribunal. Without taking any ground in the memo of appeal filed before the First Appellate authority or the Tribunal below the assessee or department is not legally entitled to formulate any new question of law in the reference application. 

22. Similar view has been taken by the Indian Supreme Court in the case of Commissioner of Income Tax, Bombay vs Indian Steam Navigation Company, reported in AIR 1961 SC 163 = 42 ITR 589 (SC) wherein Supreme Court of India enunciated the following principle regarding "question of law arising out of such order" and held that; 
"(1) When a question is raised before the Appellate Tribunal and is dealt with by, it is clearly one arising out of its order; 
(2) When a question of law is raised before the Tribunal but the Tribunal fails to deal with it, it must be deemed to have been dealt with by it, and is, therefore, one arising out of its order. 
(3) When a question is not raised before the Tribunal but the Tribunal deals with it that will also be a question arising out of its order. 
(4) When a question of law is neither raised before the Tribunal nor considered by it, it will not be a question arising out of its order notwithstanding that it may arise on the finding given by it. 
Stating the position compendiously, it is only a question that has been raised before or decided by the Tribunal that could be held to arise out of its order. ILR (1949) Born 174: AIR 1949 Bom 24: (1948) 16 ITR 227 & ILR (1953) Nag 253: AIR 1953 Nag 173: 1952-22 1TR 448. Over ruled."

23. Subsequently the Supreme Court of India echosed the above view in the case of Estate of the late AMKM Karuppan Chettiar vs Commissioner of Income Tax (1969), 72 ITR 403 (SC) 407 and held as under; 
"Before the Tribunal also that contention was raised—The question was raised before the Tribunal. Even if it was not expressly dealt with by the Tribunal, it still arose out of the order of the Tribunal" 

24. Now, what question has arisen out of the order of the Tribunal is well settled and our Apex Court in the case of Dhaka Vegetable Oil Industries Ltd. vs Commissioner of Taxes, Dhaka (North) Zone, Dhaka reported in 49 DLR (AD) 136 also concurred the above view of the Indian Supreme Court and held as under: "In any view of the matter, it is well settled that whether a question of law is answered by the tribunal or not if the question is raised before the tribunal then it will be taken to have arisen from its order". 

25. In the absence of relevant documents as referred to in section 160(2) of the Income Tax Ordinance, 1984 if this court give any answer to the question raised in the reference application either in affirmative or in negative, the same will be mechanical exercise of power. Therefore, we hold that this court is only bound to answer the question raised in the reference application either in affirmative or in negative subject to compliance of the provision of section 160 (2) of the said Ordinance by the applicant and that the question raised in the reference application filed under section 160(1) of the said Ordinance has arisen out of the order passed by the Tribunal. 

26. Since the First Appellate Authority and the Taxes Appellate Tribunal did not exercise discretion properly as stated above following law, we are of the view that the case should be sent back to the Tax Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench 3, Dhaka to consider the documents afresh filed before this court and the 
DCT. The assessee applicant is also at liberty to produce those documents before the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench 3, Dhaka. 

27. Therefore, we answer the question raised in the reference application in negative and set-aside the order dated 29-10-2015 passed by the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench 3, Dhaka in Income Tax Appeal No. 165 of 2015-2016 (Assessment year 2014-2015) and the case is sent back on remand to the Taxes Appellate Tribunal, Division Bench 3, Dhaka to consider the documents as mentioned above and decide the case afresh following the observations and findings made herein above. 

However, there will be no order as to costs. 

End.
